  "rating": {
    "inherent": "moderate",
    "residual": "moderate",
    "forecast": "high",
    "rationale": "Inherent risk is driven by two moderate findings: internal policy content reaching the chat reply (F1, internal source, S2 egress sink) and the complete absence of an attributable record of employee questions and agent answers (F3, property finding, repudiation). A pen-test-verified network egress block, a recipient-bound reply destination, a non-rendering chat client, and a least-privilege read-only credential bring F1's residual down to low; F3 has no control that restores attributable provenance, so its residual stays moderate. The forecast ceiling of high comes from F3: if autonomy increases or the policy index scope expands to confidential content while no audit log exists, the accountability and disclosure gap becomes materially worse. No complete path from an untrusted source to any sink was found; this design has no web, email, or other externally authored input."
  },
  "limitations": [
    "This assessment reflects the declared design and configuration described in the provided artifacts (system prompt, tool manifest, and runtime notes), not observed runtime behavior; declared controls have not been independently verified except where the runtime notes state pen-test verification (network egress denial).",
    "No explicit transport-security statement was provided for the chat channel carrying employee questions and replies; confidentiality and integrity in transit are assumed but not confirmed.",
    "max_unattended_steps and max_runtime_minutes were not stated explicitly; a bound of 2 steps (one search call, one reply) was inferred from the system prompt's single search-then-answer structure, which lowers confidence on the autonomy-modifier calculation.",
    "No information was provided on whether policy_search query logs are visible to staff beyond the requesting employee, or on whether policy authorship/review is recorded in a tamper-evident log."
  ]
